WEBVTT

NOTE Narration by an AI voice: Matilda (XrExE9yKIg1WjnnlVkGX), eleven_v4.

intro
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:10.300
A password-spray alert has been quiet for weeks. Here GitHub Copilot CLI hunts for a slow spray with Splunk and the Machine Data Lake.

ask
00:00:10.600 --> 00:00:18.040
The hunter asks Copilot to check the last fourteen days of sign-ins and name the source that looks like a spray.

searches
00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:26.400
Copilot queries the hot index through Splunk MCP: failures per source, then the busiest five minutes.

why-quiet
00:00:27.800 --> 00:00:36.280
That five-minute check matters. A spray that tries each account once never trips an alert on a hundred failures.

suspect
00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:43.500
One address stands out: over five hundred failures on three hundred accounts.

vpn
00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:50.680
The busy VPN fails too, but mostly succeeds: the false positive to avoid.

notebook
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:56.200
Copilot opens the hunt notebook, shown on the right, and sets the IP.

lake-ask
00:00:56.600 --> 00:01:00.120
Lake searches cost more, so it asks.

lake-approved
00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:08.200
Approved, the raw search pulls this address's full history, a hundred and twenty days.

history
00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:19.320
The index saw ten days. The lake shows a September campaign in amber, a quiet October, then a return.

table-approved
00:01:21.900 --> 00:01:28.540
Copilot loads the ninety-day analytics table, shared through Open Sharing, straight into pandas.

table-why
00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:33.540
Only the columns the hunt needs travel, so it loads in seconds.

table-result
00:01:33.900 --> 00:01:38.380
A hundred and four thousand sign-ins, the suspect on top.

backtest-approved
00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:47.520
The backtest runs the new rule over every sign-in in the kernel. Nothing is written to Splunk.

rule
00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:58.160
It flags only the suspect, on thirteen days. Each dot is one address on one day, and the busy VPN stays under the line.

intel
00:01:58.600 --> 00:02:06.680
Enrichment finds it in a community threat feed, and a successful sign-in to a privileged backup account at three in the morning.

finding
00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:13.740
Copilot sums up: contain the backup account, block the address, deploy the detection.

traceable
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:20.040
Every claim traces back to a notebook cell, so the hunt is easy to review.

leave
00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:25.760
Then the hunter leaves Copilot and opens the executed notebook in the terminal.

record
00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:33.580
The executed notebook is the record, ending in the daily search to save as the alert.

value-data
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:41.280
Splunk keeps fourteen hot days fast, and the Machine Data Lake keeps months of history cheaply.

value-agent
00:02:41.700 --> 00:02:49.380
An agent turns that history into a tested detection in minutes, with a person approving every costly step.

value-close
00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:52.760
Faster hunts, fewer missed attacks.
